ets/b1367f748639ca013924/page-1.jpg","width":1445,"height":1870,"method":"openai-vision:gpt-5.4-mini","status":"complete"},{"number":2,"image":"/repeat-v2/assets/b1367f748639ca013924/page-2.jpg","width":1445,"height":1870,"method":"openai-vision:gpt-5.4-mini","status":"complete"}],"questions":[{"id":"b1367f748639ca013924-q-1","number":"1A","markdown":"The following table gives the distances in nautical miles of the horizon for the given heights in feet above the earth's surface:\n\n| $x$ = height | 100 | 150 | 200 | 250 | 300 | 350 | 400 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| $y$ = distance | 10.63 | 13.03 | 15.04 | 16.81 | 18.42 | 19.9 | 21.27 |\n\nFind the approximate value of $y$ when $x = 118$ ft.","marks":4,"pageNumbers":[1],"diagrams":[],"sourceImages":[{"src":"/repeat-v2/assets/b1367f748639ca013924/q-1-p-1.jpg","pageNumber":1,"bbox":[0,558,1445,181]}],"confidence":"high","reviewNotes":[]},{"id":"b1367f748639ca013924-q-2","number":"1B","markdown":"Solve\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{y^3}\\,dx + \\frac{y^2 - 3x^2}{y^4}\\,dy = 0.\n$$","marks":3,"pageNumbers":[1],"diagrams":[],"sourceImages":[{"src":"/repeat-v2/assets/b1367f748639ca013924/q-2-p-1.jpg","pageNumber":1,"bbox":[0,731,1445,136]}],"confidence":"high","reviewNotes":[]},{"id":"b1367f748639ca013924-q-3","number":"1C","markdown":"Solve\n\n$$\n(x^3y^2 + xy)\\,dx = dy.\n$$","marks":3,"pageNumbers":[1],"diagrams":[],"sourceImages":[{"src":"/repeat-v2/assets/b1367f748639ca013924/q-3-p-1.jpg","pageNumber":1,"bbox":[0,859,1445,85]}],"confidence":"high","reviewNotes":[]},{"id":"b1367f748639ca013924-q-4","number":"2A","markdown":"Use Runge-Kutta method of order four to find an approximate value of $y$ at $x = 0.1, 0.2$, given\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\sqrt{x+y},\\ y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nTake $h=0.1$, correct to four decimal places.","marks":4,"pageNumbers":[1],"diagrams":[],"sourceImages":[{"src":"/repeat-v2/assets/b1367f748639ca013924/q-4-p-1.jpg","pageNumber":1,"bbox":[0,936,1445,124]}],"confidence":"high","reviewNotes":[]},{"id":"b1367f748639ca013924-q-5","number":"2B","markdown":"Solve \n\n$$\n(D^2 - 4D + 3)y = \\sin 3x\\cos 2x.\n$$","marks":3,"pageNumbers":[1],"diagrams":[],"sourceImages":[{"src":"/repeat-v2/assets/b1367f748639ca013924/q-5-p-1.jpg","pageNumber":1,"bbox":[0,1052,1445,75]}],"confidence":"high","reviewNotes":[]},{"id":"b1367f748639ca013924-q-6","number":"2C","markdown":"Solve\n\n$$\n\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} - y = \\frac{2}{1+e^x}\n$$\n\nby using method of variation of parameters.","marks":3,"pageNumbers":[1],"diagrams":[],"sourceImages":[{"src":"/repeat-v2/assets/b1367f748639ca013924/q-6-p-1.jpg","pageNumber":1,"bbox":[0,1119,1445,137]}],"confidence":"high","reviewNotes":[]},{"id":"b1367f748639ca013924-q-7","number":"3A","markdown":"Find all the eigenvalues and any two of the eigenvectors of the matrix\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}\n6 & -2 & 2\\\\\n-2 & 3 & -1\\\\\n2 & -1 & 3\n\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$","marks":4,"pageNumbers":[1],"diagrams":[],"sourceImages":[{"src":"/repeat-v2/assets/b1367f748639ca013924/q-7-p-1.jpg","pageNumber":1,"bbox":[0,1248,1445,196]}],"confidence":"high","reviewNotes":[]},{"id":"b1367f748639ca013924-q-8","number":"3B","markdown":"Use Newton's divided difference formula to find $f(4)$ from the following data:\n\n| $x$ | 0 | 2 | 3 | 6 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| $f(x)$ | -4 | 2 | 14 | 158 |","marks":3,"pageNumbers":[1],"diagrams":[],"sourceImages":[{"src":"/repeat-v2/assets/b1367f748639ca013924/q-8-p-1.jpg","pageNumber":1,"bbox":[0,1436,1445,224]}],"confidence":"high","reviewNotes":[]},{"id":"b1367f748639ca013924-q-9","number":"3C","markdown":"Use Trapezoidal rule to find an approximate value of\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\pi \\sqrt{\\sin\\theta}\\,d\\theta\n$$\n\nby taking 6 equal subintervals, correct to 4 decimal places.","marks":3,"pageNumbers":[1],"diagrams":[],"sourceImages":[{"src":"/repeat-v2/assets/b1367f748639ca013924/q-9-p-1.jpg","pageNumber":1,"bbox":[0,1652,1445,116]}],"confidence":"high","reviewNotes":[]},{"id":"b1367f748639ca013924-q-10","number":"4A","markdown":"Use Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization process to construct an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ form the set of vectors $\\{(0,0,1),\\ (1,2,0),\\ (8,1,-6)\\}.$","marks":4,"pageNumbers":[2],"diagrams":[],"sourceImages":[{"src":"/repeat-v2/assets/b1367f748639ca013924/q-10-p-2.jpg","pageNumber":2,"bbox":[0,83,1445,146]}],"confidence":"high","reviewNotes":["The printed word appears as 'form' in the source; likely intended 'from'."]},{"id":"b1367f748639ca013924-q-11","number":"4B","markdown":"Use Euler's modified method to find an approximate value of $y(0.1)$, given\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx} = x^2 + y,\\ y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nTake $h=0.05$, correct to three decimal places.","marks":3,"pageNumbers":[2],"diagrams":[],"sourceImages":[{"src":"/repeat-v2/assets/b1367f748639ca013924/q-11-p-2.jpg","pageNumber":2,"bbox":[0,221,1445,120]}],"confidence":"high","reviewNotes":[]},{"id":"b1367f748639ca013924-q-12","number":"4C","markdown":"Use Newton-Raphson method, to find a real root of the equation $e^x - 3x = 0$ by taking the initial approximate root as 0.5, correct to four decimal places.","marks":3,"pageNumbers":[2],"diagrams":[],"sourceImages":[{"src":"/repeat-v2/assets/b1367f748639ca013924/q-12-p-2.jpg","pageNumber":2,"bbox":[0,333,1445,119]}],"confidence":"high","reviewNotes":[]},{"id":"b1367f748639ca013924-q-13","number":"5A","markdown":"Prove that the set $S$ of non-zero orthogonal vectors in an inner product space $X$ is linearly independent.","marks":4,"pageNumbers":[2],"diagrams":[],"sourceImages":[{"src":"/repeat-v2/assets/b1367f748639ca013924/q-13-p-2.jpg","pageNumber":2,"bbox":[0,444,1445,85]}],"confidence":"high","reviewNotes":[]},{"id":"b1367f748639ca013924-q-14","number":"5B","markdown":"For what value of $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ the following set of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx+y+z &= 6\\\\\nx+2y+3z &= 10\\\\\nx+2y+\\lambda z &= \\mu\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhave\n\ni) Infinitely many solutions\n\nii) Unique solutions\n\niii) No solution","marks":3,"pageNumbers":[2],"diagrams":[],"sourceImages":[{"src":"/repeat-v2/assets/b1367f748639ca013924/q-14-p-2.jpg","pageNumber":2,"bbox":[0,521,1445,422]}],"confidence":"high","reviewNotes":[]},{"id":"b1367f748639ca013924-q-15","number":"5C","markdown":"Use Jacobi's method to solve the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n83x + 11y - 4z &= 95\\\\\n7x + 52y + 13z &= 104\\\\\n3x + 8y + 29z &= 71\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith initial approximation as $x^{(0)} = y^{(0)} = z^{(0)} = 0$.\nCarry out four iterations, correct to four decimal places.","marks":3,"pageNumbers":[2],"diagrams":[],"sourceImages":[{"src":"/repeat-v2/assets/b1367f748639ca013924/q-15-p-2.jpg","pageNumber":2,"bbox":[0,935,1445,833]}],"confidence":"high","reviewNotes":[]}],"warnings":["Transcribed from original page images with gpt-5.4-mini. Automatic transcription is not independent human verification."]}
